WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:01.980
Instructor: All right, so I'd like to demonstrate

00:01.980 --> 00:03.330
using "Git Fetch."

00:03.330 --> 00:04.950
And in order to do that,

00:04.950 --> 00:08.550
I need to have some changes on a GitHub repo

00:08.550 --> 00:10.380
that I don't have locally.

00:10.380 --> 00:13.260
Normally, this would come from collaborators, right,

00:13.260 --> 00:14.520
from other people I'm working with,

00:14.520 --> 00:16.740
who are pushing their own changes up.

00:16.740 --> 00:20.700
But right now, if it's just me, I kind of need to fake that,

00:20.700 --> 00:24.180
or at least change the GitHub repo in another way.

00:24.180 --> 00:25.560
And I can do that.

00:25.560 --> 00:27.330
You can make any GitHub repo

00:27.330 --> 00:31.050
and manually add files and edit files.

00:31.050 --> 00:32.520
So that's what I'm gonna do.

00:32.520 --> 00:34.590
I'm gonna go on GitHub.

00:34.590 --> 00:38.100
So first of all, I'm working on this "remote-branches-demo"

00:38.100 --> 00:39.540
where I've got the ASCII art.

00:39.540 --> 00:43.290
So I've got the fantasy branch, we added that phoenix into,

00:43.290 --> 00:46.590
and then I've got what, four other, three other branches.

00:46.590 --> 00:48.390
Let's go to the food branch,

00:48.390 --> 00:52.080
and I'm gonna create a new file on GitHub.

00:52.080 --> 00:54.270
I won't have this on my machine,

00:54.270 --> 00:56.157
so I can "create new file."

00:57.540 --> 00:59.952
And then I'm using this ASCII Art website.

00:59.952 --> 01:03.990
I'm just gonna take this apple and paste it in here.

01:03.990 --> 01:06.630
So if you're following along or doing something similar,

01:06.630 --> 01:08.880
you can edit any file on GitHub,

01:08.880 --> 01:10.620
assuming it's in a repo you own.

01:10.620 --> 01:13.980
And when you do that, as you can see here,

01:13.980 --> 01:15.930
it's gonna generate a commit.

01:15.930 --> 01:17.880
And I also need to come up with a name for the file.

01:17.880 --> 01:20.070
So I'll do "apple.txt,"

01:20.070 --> 01:23.610
and then I'll create it on the food branch.

01:23.610 --> 01:25.650
I could create a new branch, I'm not gonna do that.

01:25.650 --> 01:27.603
I'm adding this to the food branch.

01:29.190 --> 01:32.850
Okay, so now it shows just like any other commit.

01:32.850 --> 01:35.850
I have a new file, "create apple.txt."

01:35.850 --> 01:37.680
It's showing up right here.

01:37.680 --> 01:40.140
I'm gonna switch now to a different branch.

01:40.140 --> 01:42.630
How about to the movies branch?

01:42.630 --> 01:46.290
And I'll copy this Tinkerbell ASCII Art,

01:46.290 --> 01:49.500
and put that in a file on the movies branch

01:49.500 --> 01:51.937
called Tinkerbell.

01:51.937 --> 01:56.040
"tinkerbell.txt," and I'll paste that in,

01:56.040 --> 01:58.740
and I'll commit again to make a new file

01:58.740 --> 02:00.300
on the movies branch.

02:00.300 --> 02:02.460
So all of this that I've done so far,

02:02.460 --> 02:04.320
it has nothing to do with fetching,

02:04.320 --> 02:06.180
we're just updating the repo

02:06.180 --> 02:08.670
so that now we have stuff on GitHub

02:08.670 --> 02:11.670
that I don't have present on my local machine.

02:11.670 --> 02:13.920
And again, normally the way this would happen

02:13.920 --> 02:15.810
is that somebody pushes up changes

02:15.810 --> 02:19.560
from their own local repo, if I had collaborators.

02:19.560 --> 02:23.520
All right, so now we have three files on movies,

02:23.520 --> 02:26.070
and we have three files in food.

02:26.070 --> 02:29.100
Locally, let's start with the movies branch.

02:29.100 --> 02:31.500
If I go to the movies branch here,

02:31.500 --> 02:33.787
so just make sure I don't have any changes here.

02:33.787 --> 02:35.637
"git switch" to "movies."

02:36.900 --> 02:40.020
All right? I just have two files, right?

02:40.020 --> 02:42.180
If I type "git status," it says I'm up to date

02:42.180 --> 02:43.800
with origin/movies.

02:43.800 --> 02:45.810
But that just means I'm up to date

02:45.810 --> 02:50.550
with what my local repo knows origin/movies was,

02:50.550 --> 02:54.510
not what it exactly is at this one moment in time,

02:54.510 --> 02:56.130
because it can change.

02:56.130 --> 02:58.897
My repo is not constantly asking, "Hey, is there new stuff?

02:58.897 --> 03:00.510
"Is there new stuff from GitHub?"

03:00.510 --> 03:01.440
I have to do that.

03:01.440 --> 03:05.700
I have to tell it, "Go fetch changes, go fetch the latest."

03:05.700 --> 03:07.740
So, that's what I wanna do right now.

03:07.740 --> 03:08.940
I want to get the changes.

03:08.940 --> 03:11.973
Let's say my friend told me, what'd I say his name was?

03:12.810 --> 03:15.487
Bob Head? Yeah, Bob Head told me,

03:15.487 --> 03:18.397
"Colta just added to the movies branch.

03:18.397 --> 03:20.610
"Go fetch the changes, you need them."

03:20.610 --> 03:22.320
So "git fetch,"

03:22.320 --> 03:26.310
and then I can specify origin to get all changes,

03:26.310 --> 03:30.769
or I can specify one branch, like movies.

03:30.769 --> 03:33.630
Normally I just do "git fetch origin"

03:33.630 --> 03:37.950
or just even "git fetch" because it will default to origin.

03:37.950 --> 03:40.500
But if you only want to fetch one branch

03:40.500 --> 03:42.300
then you can specify.

03:42.300 --> 03:45.480
So I'm gonna do a "git fetch,"
(keys tapping)

03:45.480 --> 03:47.790
and I'll do "origin," just to be clearer.

03:47.790 --> 03:50.317
We only have one remote, but here we go.

03:50.317 --> 03:51.900
"git fetch origin."

03:51.900 --> 03:54.720
Okay, doesn't seem like a whole lot happened, right?

03:54.720 --> 03:56.370
Well let's type "git status" now.

03:57.210 --> 03:59.850
Now I see a completely different message.

03:59.850 --> 04:02.527
It says, "Your branch is behind 'origin/movies'

04:02.527 --> 04:04.230
"by 1 commit."

04:04.230 --> 04:08.790
So we can tell origin/movies, that remote branch reference,

04:08.790 --> 04:10.110
has been updated.

04:10.110 --> 04:12.780
It's the equivalent with different branch names

04:12.780 --> 04:13.680
of what we saw here

04:13.680 --> 04:15.600
where we had master.

04:15.600 --> 04:18.510
origin/master was updated when we fetched

04:18.510 --> 04:20.070
to point to something new,

04:20.070 --> 04:22.710
but my branch itself is unchanged.

04:22.710 --> 04:25.680
So same idea, but swap in movies.

04:25.680 --> 04:27.660
My movies branch is here,

04:27.660 --> 04:31.470
and origin/movies is pointing at a new commit.

04:31.470 --> 04:34.080
So if I go back, let's take a look at our files.

04:34.080 --> 04:36.330
Just two files in my working directory.

04:36.330 --> 04:38.610
I don't have that new work.

04:38.610 --> 04:43.610
If I type "git log," I don't see that new commit either.

04:44.340 --> 04:47.220
Nothing has changed in my working directory.

04:47.220 --> 04:50.970
However, origin/movies is now pointing to something new.

04:50.970 --> 04:52.290
It's pointing to a new commit,

04:52.290 --> 04:53.940
and I can take a look at that.

04:53.940 --> 04:58.940
I can do "git checkout origin/movies."

04:59.070 --> 05:00.360
I just wanna take a peek.

05:00.360 --> 05:03.420
What is the work that Bob Head did?

05:03.420 --> 05:05.373
And now I enter detached HEAD.

05:06.540 --> 05:10.470
And now I see, oh yeah, Tinkerbell is here, right?

05:10.470 --> 05:12.600
Ghostbusters is here, Bambi.

05:12.600 --> 05:14.460
But this is the new work.

05:14.460 --> 05:16.890
Okay, and if I type "git log" here,

05:16.890 --> 05:20.370
we see "Create tinkerbell," that was the most recent commit,

05:20.370 --> 05:22.770
because we are looking at origin/movies.

05:22.770 --> 05:25.560
So I could make a new branch from here if I wanted to.

05:25.560 --> 05:27.570
But what I'm actually gonna show you next

05:27.570 --> 05:30.990
is how we can use the "pull" command to help us out,

05:30.990 --> 05:32.820
if we actually want to integrate these changes

05:32.820 --> 05:34.410
into our working directory.

05:34.410 --> 05:37.620
But for now fetch is just getting those changes from GitHub,

05:37.620 --> 05:39.570
or any other remote repo,

05:39.570 --> 05:42.030
and it brings them down to my machine.

05:42.030 --> 05:43.620
It just doesn't integrate them.

05:43.620 --> 05:45.960
So I can switch branches, too.

05:45.960 --> 05:49.470
Let's make sure I get back out of detached HEAD.

05:49.470 --> 05:51.545
So let's switch back to movies.

05:51.545 --> 05:53.370
(keys tapping)

05:53.370 --> 05:56.820
Okay, so I'm still behind origin/movies by one.

05:56.820 --> 06:01.293
Now let's switch to, what was it, foods? No, food.

06:02.520 --> 06:05.550
All right. And oh we didn't even have food

06:05.550 --> 06:09.330
on my machine at this point, I hadn't actually worked on it.

06:09.330 --> 06:12.570
So I just saw a message, I cleared it unfortunately.

06:12.570 --> 06:14.700
But it said it set up the branch for me.

06:14.700 --> 06:17.130
And if I take a look at what we have,

06:17.130 --> 06:20.460
it's already up to date, it has that apple file.

06:20.460 --> 06:22.830
So that's kind of a unique case

06:22.830 --> 06:25.980
because I hadn't even bothered looking at this branch yet,

06:25.980 --> 06:29.190
but because I switched to it for the very first time,

06:29.190 --> 06:30.510
and I had just fetched,

06:30.510 --> 06:33.690
it used the most recent information that it fetched.

06:33.690 --> 06:36.270
From, remember we have these remote tracking branches.

06:36.270 --> 06:40.830
Remember, "git branch -r." It used origin/food.

06:40.830 --> 06:42.360
So I'm gonna make one more change.

06:42.360 --> 06:44.820
Let's do another food.

06:44.820 --> 06:48.213
So we'll go back to food here, and I'll add a new file,

06:49.440 --> 06:53.280
and I guess I'll take this, is it tea, coffee,

06:53.280 --> 06:55.020
whatever this is.

06:55.020 --> 06:59.460
We'll put that, we'll call this, yeah, "coffee.txt."

06:59.460 --> 07:01.890
So I'm making a new file on the food branch.

07:01.890 --> 07:02.793
I'll commit.

07:04.680 --> 07:05.670
Okay.

07:05.670 --> 07:07.950
And now I'm gonna go back here,

07:07.950 --> 07:11.010
and if I type "git status" on the food branch,

07:11.010 --> 07:12.180
it thinks it's up to date.

07:12.180 --> 07:16.020
It is up to date with what we have in origin/food.

07:16.020 --> 07:18.420
But I can get more recent information,

07:18.420 --> 07:19.980
more up-to-date information.

07:19.980 --> 07:22.920
This time I'll just show fetching a particular branch.

07:22.920 --> 07:24.697
It will work exactly the same way.

07:24.697 --> 07:27.237
"git fetch origin food."

07:28.590 --> 07:31.680
And what we see now is a decent chunk of output,

07:31.680 --> 07:34.237
and if I type "git status" it tells me,

07:34.237 --> 07:36.577
"Hey, you're on this branch called food,

07:36.577 --> 07:40.290
"and it's behind origin/food By 1 commit."

07:40.290 --> 07:42.660
Just a moment ago before I fetched, it was up to date.

07:42.660 --> 07:44.850
Now it says you're behind by one.

07:44.850 --> 07:47.250
So it tells me, "use git pull to update your branch."

07:47.250 --> 07:49.770
That's a sneak peek at what we're gonna cover next.

07:49.770 --> 07:51.397
But as you just saw,

07:51.397 --> 07:53.940
"git fetch" got me the latest information

07:53.940 --> 07:55.110
from that food branch,

07:55.110 --> 07:56.820
and now we can take a look at it.

07:56.820 --> 07:58.530
So if I inspect,

07:58.530 --> 08:01.050
right, we're not looking at the latest version,

08:01.050 --> 08:02.940
we're not looking at what we just fetched.

08:02.940 --> 08:04.650
This is my working directory.

08:04.650 --> 08:09.650
But now if I check it out, "origin/food."

08:10.710 --> 08:15.210
Detached HEAD, and we see coffee is here, all right?

08:15.210 --> 08:17.730
So that's a basic idea of fetching.

08:17.730 --> 08:19.080
There are other things, you know,

08:19.080 --> 08:20.790
that you would use fetch for.

08:20.790 --> 08:22.410
It's not just about updating

08:22.410 --> 08:24.060
the particular branch that you're on

08:24.060 --> 08:25.530
and getting the latest.

08:25.530 --> 08:28.890
Also, it might be that a new branch was created.

08:28.890 --> 08:30.090
That's really common.

08:30.090 --> 08:32.790
I can make a new branch here just as a demonstration.

08:32.790 --> 08:36.150
I'll delete it, but I can make one called,

08:36.150 --> 08:40.140
how about "morefood," okay?

08:40.140 --> 08:43.410
Create that branch, I'm doing it on GitHub.

08:43.410 --> 08:44.940
So I just made this branch.

08:44.940 --> 08:46.500
It's only on GitHub.

08:46.500 --> 08:47.940
I don't know about it here.

08:47.940 --> 08:50.430
If I do "git branch -r,"

08:50.430 --> 08:52.020
I don't see "morefood."

08:52.020 --> 08:55.197
But as soon as I fetch, "git fetch,"

08:56.040 --> 08:59.790
now, it says, "Oh, there's this thing, origin/morefood."

08:59.790 --> 09:01.230
We know about it now.

09:01.230 --> 09:06.230
So if I do "git branch -r" again, now I have "morefood."

09:06.660 --> 09:08.490
It's exactly the same as my food branch,

09:08.490 --> 09:09.630
I didn't change it in any way,

09:09.630 --> 09:11.280
so we wouldn't see a difference.

09:11.280 --> 09:14.700
But now my computer, my local repo, knows about it.

09:14.700 --> 09:17.640
A moment ago, it didn't know about it until I fetched.

09:17.640 --> 09:20.310
So using "fetch" gets you the latest information

09:20.310 --> 09:22.230
about some remote repository.

09:22.230 --> 09:24.690
It just doesn't automatically integrate those changes

09:24.690 --> 09:26.290
into whatever you're working on.
